  "query_preview": "..\n\nAnother pleasant late August day under quickly thickening high clouds \nis in progress for SE Mi as high pressure centered on the Atlantic \ncoast extends back into the Great Lakes. The high pressure air mass \nhas carried temperatures on the mild side with low humidity over the \nlast few days. This begins to change tonight as a broad area of low \npressure developing across the Plains increasingly becomes the \nforecast concentration for the Great Lakes Sunday through the early \nweek period. \n\nFirst up is the MCS decaying nearby upstream this afternoon and the \npotential for remnants surviving into Lower Mi this evening, \nfollowed by a new round of nocturnal convection later tonight. \nAfternoon satellite and radar trends offer convincing evidence of \nMCS dissipation, but also of a robust MCV produced as a result. This \nwave is captured well by the range of deterministic models and is \nprojected to move into the area with a chance of carrying remnants \nthis far east or producing new scattered showers overhead this \nevening. Model depictions of the mid level theta-e ridge indicate \nslightly improved potential toward the Tri Cities and northern Thumb \nfollowed by a period of subsidence trailing the MCV heading into the \npost midnight hours. The Tri Cities and Thumb, down toward the I-69 \ncorridor, is also the area favored for another round of \nshowers/storms that move in from the Midwest late tonight and Sunday \nmorning. This is convection forced by the low level jet more nearby \nto the west compared to this morning, from WI into western Lower Mi, \nwhich then follows the 850 mb theta-e ridge inbound ahead of the \nsystem warm front into central and SE Mi. The convective signal is \nconvincing in both HREF/REFS ensemble mean QPF and in that from \ndeterministic runs as well due to the involvement of the larger \nscale low pressure system. NBM rain probabilities are much improved \nin this model cycle offering chance POPs this evening trending to \nlikely/numerous late tonight and categorical/widespread Sunday \nmorning north of metro Detroit. No concerns for severe intensity \ngiven the considerable west to east instability gradient, however \nthere is a low chance of localized heavy rainfall in the west to \neast alignment of convective development and downstream propagation. \n\nThe same is true for Sunday as low pressure moves toward the \nSD/IA/MN border and the warm front extends eastward into southern \nLower Mi by Sunday afternoon. The position of the warm front \nmaintains cloudy conditions all areas and showers again having \ngreater coverage with northward extent from metro Detroit toward the \nTri Cities and northern Thumb. HREF and REFS mean SB and MU CAPE are \nbelow 500 J/kg while mean PW approaches 2 inches through peak \nheating, limiting convective potential to an additional chance for \nlocally heavy rain (see Hydro below) Sunday afternoon and Sunday \nnight.    \n\nThere was a slight edge toward model solutions moving the warm front\ninto the northern Great Lakes for a lengthy period of time Monday \nafternoon and Tuesday, allowing a new round of heat and humidity to \nbuild into the region. Trends in today's model cycle are less \nfavorable for full development of a warm sector into SE Mi during \nthis time. Instead, model solutions suggest a flatter mid/upper level\nridge and faster westerly flow aloft over the Great Lakes leading to\nquick progression of surface low pressure and arrival of the \ntrailing cold front. This scenario both shortens the residence time \nof any potential heat and humidity while increasing the chance for \nclouds and additional showers/storms as the front stalls until \nperhaps Wednesday.",
